WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.940
Hey everybody, welcome
back to Firm Learning.

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.970
My name is Heinrich, and it's great

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:06.760
that all of you are there and ready

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.230
to watch my second video on this channel.

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:12.100
I'm still super excited
to have done this at all

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.390
and be able now to share
my experience with you,

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.380
my experiences from my background

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:19.150
in business and management.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:21.270
So I thought long and
hard about what could be

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:23.760
a good topic for this video,

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.280
and I think one thing that many, many,

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.970
many new joiners in business struggle with

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:31.440
is behavior in meetings.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:32.630
And what do I mean that?

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:35.420
Well, you can imagine most corporations,

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.170
we have many, many meetings every day.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.010
You probably meet with your team lead,

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:41.510
then with other colleagues
from other departments,

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:43.570
you sit together to discuss topics

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:45.870
and make advancements in the projects

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.760
that you're pursuing and
to basically collect input

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.130
from other people you're working with.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:53.020
And recently, a more junior colleague

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:54.920
at my workplace approached me

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.350
and asked me about
specifically this topic.

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.590
He told me, Heinrich, I'm
sitting in these meetings,

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:03.640
very often I do not
know what really to say.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.920
Is it the right thing for
me as a junior colleague

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.610
to say anything at all?

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:10.350
Should I just be there,
listen to the others?

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.420
What is my role, what am I expected to do

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:15.870
as a junior colleague in these meetings?

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.280
And I could just very
much relate with that.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.770
I clearly remember when I
was a rookie in consulting

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:24.560
and I was sitting in these first meetings

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.500
with some senior clients.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.590
I felt highly intimidated.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:30.560
You're very often not sitting there alone

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.430
but then there is your project lead,

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.630
your engagement manager,
there is a partner

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:37.320
from your consulting firm there,

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.810
full care leader, might
also be a junior partner

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.280
and then a whole bunch of clients.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.200
And there was, of course, me as well,

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:48.770
Heinrich, a junior consultant,
new on the team, a rookie,

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:51.040
and I just didn't have
a clue what to do, okay?

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.210
And I think this is
just the normal reaction

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.390
that many people have when they are facing

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:57.620
these kinds of situations.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.770
So reflecting on that
experience and now looking back,

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:02.660
I think there are three tips that I would

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:05.600
like to share with you, what
you as a junior colleague

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.800
actually can do to have
a positive contribution

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:12.400
to the meeting and also to
be perceived a professional.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.000
The first tip I'd like to give you

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.000
is to really clarify with your
team lead what your role is.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.470
Go to him and ask him, okay,
we're having this meeting,

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.320
I see that I'm part of
the list of participants,

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:26.990
what do you expect me to do?

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:28.730
What is your role?

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:30.190
What shall I do, right?

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:33.110
And one role could be
that you will moderate,

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:35.090
you will lead through the meetings,

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:36.640
you will lead through the agenda,

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.460
you will call up the different topics,

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.660
make sure that the time is kept

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:42.620
that you are covering
all the topics you want

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.140
to discuss in the meeting.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:46.240
Make sure that you get the contributions

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.830
from everybody in the
room and really step up

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:51.380
by just doing a moderation kind of work.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:54.450
This could be one role, but frequently

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:58.300
this role is more for the more
senior people in the room,

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.750
usually the organizer, the person who has

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:03.530
invited to the meeting,
he will usually like

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.570
to take charge of the meeting

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:07.600
and be able to do exactly this

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.693
and just moderate the whole conversation.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:12.520
So what can you do?

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.080
And my second tip actually would be

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.120
that you can be the number guy, right?

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.940
That you can be the guy who knows

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.260
all the facts and figures about the topics

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:22.760
that you are discussing in the room.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.410
And this can relate to many, many things.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.380
So imagine you're in a sales meeting

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:31.020
and they're discussing how are
the different clients going.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.950
Maybe you're a key account manager

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:34.550
and talking about all these things

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:37.890
and suddenly a question
pops up, for instance,

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.950
what revenue did we did
last year in Asia Pacific

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:44.350
or North America or in Europe
with this client, right?

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:47.040
Or what is the revenue
of this country entity

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.766
or this local entity, what
are these people doing?

00:03:49.766 --> 00:03:50.599
And so on.

00:03:50.599 --> 00:03:53.040
And frequently these kinds of questions

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.770
are not directly being
answered by people in the room.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:57.630
People don't know the numbers by heart,

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:00.390
maybe even though they should,
but not everybody does.

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:03.400
And then basically everybody's
like, okay, let's move on.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:06.870
And here you can be the
guy who just in advance

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.800
prepares all these numbers,
has numbers like that at hand

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.570
and then can just read them out.

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:13.660
And this is something that
you as a junior colleague

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:16.200
can do because very often
you're sitting on the data,

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:18.110
you're doing the data analysis,

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:20.610
are working in Excel, are
crunching the numbers.

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:23.440
So you are at the source
of all of these things.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:26.720
And make use of that, this
is a power that you have

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.150
that then many people
don't have and don't do

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:31.280
because they're just further
away from the numbers,

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.600
not caring that much as you can care.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.960
So make use of that advantage
that you have there.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:41.020
And this goes further than just
having the numbers at hand,

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.610
often there are questions
which you will not be able

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:45.890
to answer right away
and also you cannot have

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:47.960
all the information out there available,

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.663
prepared in advance before the meeting,

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.080
but you can be the one
who looks it up, right?

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:55.530
Does the question come up,

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:57.220
search for it, look into your systems,

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:58.890
look into your information systems,

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:01.150
just pull the data, get it out,

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:03.000
present it to the rest of the team

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.570
because this is then really a value

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:06.150
that you can add to the conversation

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:07.840
by adding data and then being able

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.260
to directly pick up the discussion

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:12.110
where this question was being asked

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:14.280
instead of somebody who needs to go back

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.220
and then maybe later he can contribute

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:18.810
or distribute the
information to the group.

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:22.430
My third tip would be to always
push for clear next steps

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:25.260
and implications that
come out of the meeting.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:27.220
We all have been in these meetings

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:30.110
where after hours and hours of discussion

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:33.600
actually nothing is really
clear, nothing has been decided.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.700
It feels like a complete
waste of time to everybody.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:40.150
So what can you as a junior colleague do

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.610
in order to improve that
experience for everybody?

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:45.990
Well, first thing is clarify
what the objective is.

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:47.760
Before you even walk into the meeting,

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.920
talk to your team lead, talk
to the meeting organizer

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:52.680
if that is feasible and ask him,

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.660
what do we actually want
to take out of the meeting?

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:58.450
And if you feel that
especially towards the end

00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:01.860
there is no clear
objective, no clear result

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.910
towards that goal that
you wanted to achieve,

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:07.720
make sure that actions
are being taken, right?

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:11.850
Sit together and really
ask each in the room,

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:14.190
okay, what are the things
now that we want to do?

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:15.520
What are the next steps?

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.760
Maybe walk up front, go to a flip chart,

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.240
write it down what the next steps are

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:22.690
that are supposed to come out of there.

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:26.910
Ask the very simple question,
who was supposed to do what,

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:29.870
until when, and really make
sure that this is clear

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:32.160
and everybody is on
the same page for that.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:34.090
Because this is just the basics

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:36.210
that just need to be there after a meeting

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.460
for it to having had any value at all.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:40.170
If this is not there, it was just a waste

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:41.360
of time for everybody.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:43.610
And if other colleagues
do not understand this

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:46.180
or do not see this in
the moment but you do,

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:47.810
then for the sake of everybody,

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:50.140
step up and make sure that these things

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:51.623
are being clarified and done.

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:55.840
Of course, here you need to be
careful for office politics.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:57.930
Depending on the situation that you're in,

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:00.730
it might be that this
might seem inappropriate

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:02.340
to do as a junior colleague,

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:04.350
so here always also ask your team lead

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:05.670
or other colleagues for feedback

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:07.870
or maybe in the very beginning observe

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:10.560
how other people are
behaving just to make sure

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:13.890
that you do not break with the
kind of more implicit rules

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:16.250
of the organizations, how different people

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:19.400
are supposed to behave,
especially based on tenure

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:22.410
and just what the expectations
of the other people are

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:25.260
to not be basically seen as
an idiot by the colleagues

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:26.820
or just being seen as rude.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:29.300
This, of course, you should avoid.

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:31.860
So yeah, I think three neat
tips that are really crucial

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:33.810
and important for you to be successful

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:35.680
in meetings as a junior colleague.

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:38.620
First, clarify your role beforehand.

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:41.830
Second, in the meeting, if
there is not another role

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:44.100
that you are assigned
to, be the number guy,

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.520
be sure that you inform and distribute

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:48.930
and disseminate all
information that is there

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:50.690
and all the information
that has been asked for

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:51.690
in the meeting.

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:54.720
And third, for the sake
of everybody involved,

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:57.650
make sure that the meeting has a result.

00:07:57.650 --> 00:08:00.140
If you do this, I trust
you will be perceived

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:03.060
as a colleague who really
adds value to the meeting.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:04.550
And I trust this is the experience

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:05.930
that you will make as well.

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:08.920
So keep learning, thank you
for looking at this channel.

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:11.970
Please subscribe if you
haven't done that so far.

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:13.320
This would really mean a lot to me

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:16.060
as somebody who just
starts out with a channel

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:17.670
to see that you are actually enjoying it

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:19.640
and you are ready and only waiting

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:21.573
for new videos so that I will.

